body:
I understand your specific use-case, but I don't think a cancelled HTTP request should throw an `error`. 

Remember that HTTP in-flight requests are cancelled automatically if a new one is launched or if you manually unsubscribe from the stream, which in essence is the desired behavior.

I think we're better of if a new `HttpEvent` is implemented, which can be later accessed via the `observe` option.

``` ts
this._http.get(endpoint, {
  params,
  observe: 'events',
})
  .pipe(
    tap(ev => {
      if (ev.type === HttpEventType.Cancel) {
        // do something
      }
    })
  );
```

This check can also be easily added within a `HttpInterceptor` allowing configuration at an application wide level.

Regarding the `abort` event, I agree that the observer should be completed if the browser cancels the event.

``` ts
xhr.addEventListener('abort', () => observer.complete());
```
